  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nAs the cold front continues to push offshore, the area will remain \nunder weak, northerly flow through the afternoon. By tonight, the \nfront starts lifting back as a warm front, leading to the return of \nonshore flow. Meanwhile, some eastern Pacific vorticity lobes advect \ninto South Texas on Thursday. The REFS hints at this in the \ncomposite reflectivity guidance, while the latest NBM had silent \nPoPs (<15%). For this reason, I bumped up PoPs to 15-20% Thursday, \nbut it shouldn't be a washout as forecast accumulations are only \nexpected to be a few hundredths. By Thursday night, this PVA and a \nsurface frontal passage continue to support showers, but it will \nprimarily location along the coast. Saturday, a trough moving in \nfrom the Rockies will reinforce the northerly winds, bringing along \na broader, synoptic ascent vertical motion resulting in widespread \n20-40% PoPs, but the secondary surge will also lower PWATs to under \n1.0\" leading to low rainfall amounts Saturday night through Sunday \n(<0.5 inches). \n\nHigh temperatures today will be in the upper 70s to low 80s and \nbecoming more widespread 80s on Thursday. Changes to this trend \nbegin Saturday with highs struggling to climb above the 60s \nregion-wide Sunday. Low temperatures will drop back into the 50s \nacross the northern Brush Country/Victoria Crossroads through \nThursday morning, climbing back into the 60s before the secondary \ncold front, and then remaining in the 50s through midweek.",
